7592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1031 "Dividing Polynomials Using Synthetic Division" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1033 728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051061 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7de546e1-10a1-5134-abfc-9fe21121e7cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068593 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068593 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d90ba01-2318-5f03-9d7a-a7ad66555886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068593 #edb/ref 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} &=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 3} {x \\cdot y^2} &=\\dfrac {6} {xy^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068594 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068594 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {6a} {b^2} = x$ and $\\dfrac {3b} {2a}= y$, what is $x\\cdot y$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "702e4d33-7021-5b21-9c9d-d41463f7b665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068594 #edb/ref 1074 "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot y &= \\frac{6a}{b^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{3b}{2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6a \\cdot 3b}{b^{2} \\cdot 2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18ab}{2ab^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068595 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068595 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {(n + 1)(n + 3)} {4n^2} \\cdot \\dfrac {8n}{(n + 3)(n - 2)}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e75ba094-e762-5e8d-9175-1d9617d32128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068595 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n^{2}) \\cdot (8n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) &= ((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n \\cdot n) \\cdot (2 \\cdot 4n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{n + 1}{n} \\cdot \\frac{2}{n - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2n + 2}{n^{2} - 2n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068596 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068596 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e521a677-4e95-5c86-80c1-447924b6d34d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068596 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} &=\\dfrac {3(p - 4)} {(p + 3)(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3\\cancel{(p - 4)}} {\\cancel{(p + 3)}(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\cancel{p + 3}} {\\cancel{p - 4}} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3}{p - 3} \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068755 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068755 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068755 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068755 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying Rational Expressions With No Common Factor"